## 3. Default architecture and decision boundaries

**Selection criteria:** use free, commercially usable software; keep the number of services and credentials small; preserve durable jobs, owner approval, client isolation, and recovery; pay modest hosting/model costs when needed. This is the recommended fit for this MVP, not a claim that one stack is universally best. Model quality and server capacity still require the prompt sequence's measurements.

| Layer | Selected default | Reason and boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Public website | Semantic HTML, vanilla JavaScript, compiled Tailwind CSS | One page and a small review UI do not require React/Next.js or a separate frontend host. Compile CSS for deployment. |
| Backend | Python, FastAPI/Pydantic, Uvicorn | One codebase for validation, normalization, persistence, reviews, and delivery. Keep credentials and privileged actions server-side. |
| Workflow execution | The application's Python worker with SQLite-backed jobs | P15 already implements claims, leases, retries, and restart recovery. Reuse it; no mandatory external orchestrator, broker, Redis, or Celery. |
| Normalization | Pure Python plus `phonenumbers` | Reuse one deterministic library in the API, worker, and CLI; numbering metadata and trusted region context prevent guessed phone numbers. |
| Database | SQLite 3 through standard-library `sqlite3` | Selected for development, pilot, and initial production. No ORM or database server; follow the accepted storage contract below. |
| Client reports | Authorized CSV exports using standard-library `csv` | Importable into spreadsheets without OAuth setup or a second live datastore. Exports cannot control approval or sending. |
| Draft generation | Gemini Developer API on a paid project; initial candidate `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` | One configurable model adapter. Evaluate factual accuracy, latency, data terms, and actual cost before live use; no model tools or autonomous actions. |
| Email | Resend transactional API, initially its free plan if volume fits | One client-owned account and verified sender per pilot, with one callback endpoint for that deployment. Count notifications, replies, reminders, and alerts against quotas. |
| Hosting and HTTPS | A paid Linux VM, Caddy, and systemd | Persistent local disk, automatic TLS, and supervised application processes. Default sizing candidate: DigitalOcean Basic, 1 vCPU/2 GiB/50 GiB SSD; benchmark before committing. |
| Tests and CI | pytest + coverage.py/pytest-cov; Vitest; Playwright; Linux GitHub Actions | Free test tools for Python, JavaScript, and real browser flows; enforce the full-source 100% gates in section 9. CI service usage must fit the repository's allowance. |

Run one Uvicorn process/worker per application database. Start one durable dispatcher through the application lifespan; SQLite stores due times, claims, and progress. Recover interrupted work on startup and shut down handlers deliberately. In-memory tasks or FastAPI BackgroundTasks alone are not a durable queue. Only this backend accesses its operational database. Reuse the existing job mechanism instead of adding a generic workflow framework.

The public catalogue's service-request store belongs to this business. Each pilot has separate configuration, database, credentials, and application service. If sharing a VM initially, use distinct OS users/private directories and bind internal services to loopback; clients receive no shell access. This provides application/process separation, not independent host failure or administrator isolation. Use separate VMs when ownership or isolation requires it. A single VM has maintenance downtime and a shared failure domain; do not claim high availability.

